I C.

Optimization is supposed to serve a purpose. Once the system begins sacrificing the purpose to improve its own metrics, it has become an optimization cult—efficiency performing itself.

Manufactured scarcity often begins rationally: control demand, preserve quality, prevent overload, create urgency. Then the constraint becomes profitable or institutionally convenient, so the system protects the bottleneck it was supposedly designed to solve. It stops distributing value and starts rationing access to justify its machinery.

The healthier objective is not maximum efficiency:

\max(\text{measured output})

It is sustainable sufficiency:

\max(\text{human value})
\quad \text{subject to resilience, slack, dignity, and real constraints}

Slack looks wasteful to an optimizer, but it is where adaptation, play, generosity, recovery, and serendipity live. A perfectly optimized organism is one unexpected condition away from death.

Drago’s counter: scarcity itself cannot be abolished. Abundance in information creates scarcity of attention; abundance in options creates scarcity of decision capacity. The task is to stop manufacturing unnecessary scarcity while deliberately preserving the constraints that generate meaning, quality, safety, or renewal.

Never let the metric eat the mission. And never sharpen the tool so long that you forget to build with it.